# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# VELORA · storage/uploads/products/ — the ONE public directory under storage/
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# This is the only path in storage/ the browser is allowed to read. It is where
# the admin image pipeline writes the 1024×1024 WebP files, and it is what
# img-src 'self' in both the storefront's and the admin panel's CSP resolves.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS DIRECTORY IS THE LAST LINE OF DEFENCE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything upstream of the write is application code, and application code is
# the wrong layer to stop execution with:
#
#   · includes/uploads.php's is_safe_upload_filename() accepts only
#     [A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,64}.webp — no slash, no dot inside the stem, no null byte.
#   · the pipeline re-encodes the upload through GD and names the result
#     itself, so nothing the operator sent keeps its own extension. That removes
#     the whole .php.jpg family at the source.
#   · getimagesize() gates on a real image type, and only JPEG/PNG/GIF/WebP are
#     accepted for decoding.
#
# All of that is correct and all of it runs *before* the file exists. It decides
# whether a file is created; it cannot decide what happens to a file that is
# already here. So the question "what if something that is not an image is in
# this directory" has to be answered by the server, and this file is the answer:
#
#   · NO EXECUTION, AT ALL, OF ANY NAME. Inherited from ../../.htaccess, and
#     restated below so it does not depend on the chain being intact.
#   · SERVED BY EXTENSION WHITELIST, not by blacklist. Only .webp is readable.
#     A .png left behind by an older build, a .zip from an operator's manual
#     backup, a .htaccess copied in by a deploy tool — all 403.
#   · NO PHP PROCESSING. A request for x.webp/x.php cannot reach the
#     interpreter even if path-info handling is on and the whitelist were
#     somehow bypassed, because there is no interpreter here.
#
# Net effect: the worst a file in this directory can ever do is be downloaded.
# The inventory of secrets it protects — .env, db_schema.sql, rate-limit
# counters, client IPs, session ids — is protected by the DENY in the parents.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ─── 1. Read access: granted. Only .webp is actually served below. ───────────
<IfModule mod_authz_core.c>
  Require all granted
</IfModule>
<IfModule !mod_authz_core.c>
  Order allow,deny
  Allow from all
</IfModule>

Options -Indexes -MultiViews

# ─── 2. Execution: off, in every handler this server might be using ─────────
<IfModule mod_php.c>
  php_flag engine off
</IfModule>
<IfModule mod_php7.c>
  php_flag engine off
</IfModule>
<IfModule mod_php8.c>
  php_flag engine off
</IfModule>

RemoveHandler .php .php3 .php4 .php5 .php7 .php8 .pht .phtm .phtml .phar .pl .py .cgi .rb .sh .asp .aspx .jsp
RemoveType   .php .php3 .php4 .php5 .php7 .php8 .pht .phtm .phtml .phar
SetHandler none
SetHandler default-handler

<IfModule mod_cgi.c>
  ScriptSock /dev/null
  Options -ExecCGI
</IfModule>

# ─── 3. Extension whitelist ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `Require all granted` above opens the directory; these three lines decide
# what may actually be handed back. A whitelist, because the only extension
# this pipeline has ever written is .webp — the file is re-encoded, so its
# suffix is chosen by us and not by the uploader.
#
# FilesMatch is matched against the request path's basename, so
# `x.webp/.php` does NOT match `\.webp$` and falls through to the deny below.
<FilesMatch "\.webp$">
  <IfModule mod_authz_core.c>
    Require all granted
  </IfModule>
  <IfModule !mod_authz_core.c>
    Order allow,deny
    Allow from all
  </IfModule>
</FilesMatch>

<FilesMatch "^.*$">
  <IfModule mod_authz_core.c>
    Require all denied
  </IfModule>
  <IfModule !mod_authz_core.c>
    Order allow,deny
    Deny from all
  </IfModule>
</FilesMatch>

# ─── 4. Content-Type, stated rather than sniffed ─────────────────────────────
# X-Content-Type-Options is set globally by the document-root .htaccess as well;
# repeating it here means the guarantee does not depend on that file. The point
# of nosniff on an image directory is that a browser cannot be talked into
# treating an image as HTML, which is the other half of an upload-based XSS.
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  Header set X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
  Header set Content-Disposition "inline"
  <FilesMatch "\.webp$">
    Header set Content-Type "image/webp"
  </FilesMatch>
</IfModule>

# ─── 5. .htaccess itself must never be readable ─────────────────────────────
<FilesMatch "^\.ht">
  <IfModule mod_authz_core.c>
    Require all denied
  </IfModule>
  <IfModule !mod_authz_core.c>
    Order allow,deny
    Deny from all
  </IfModule>
</FilesMatch>